WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.000
Instructor: Okay, so recall this syntax we've seen,

00:03.000 --> 00:06.330
where we have the name of a reference, an @ sign,

00:06.330 --> 00:10.050
curly braces and then some number, so far at least.

00:10.050 --> 00:12.450
We'll stick with the numbers in this video.

00:12.450 --> 00:13.870
This is a way of referencing,

00:13.870 --> 00:16.290
let me use a different, different term.

00:16.290 --> 00:18.578
This is the way of talking about an entry

00:18.578 --> 00:21.540
in a ref log for some reference.

00:21.540 --> 00:26.010
So in other words, head @ two is the same as saying

00:26.010 --> 00:28.680
where head was two moves ago.

00:28.680 --> 00:30.930
And we can pass these references around

00:30.930 --> 00:32.460
to different commands.

00:32.460 --> 00:35.640
So one simple one I can show you is actually

00:35.640 --> 00:37.293
git ref log itself.

00:38.340 --> 00:39.210
I don't really do this

00:39.210 --> 00:41.725
but I can pass on instead of just saying everything

00:41.725 --> 00:45.290
in the head ref log, I could say head @.

00:47.130 --> 00:48.780
And then how about 10?

00:48.780 --> 00:50.220
And this will give me everything

00:50.220 --> 00:52.110
from the very beginning of the repo.

00:52.110 --> 00:55.860
So head at 58, all the way to head at 10.

00:55.860 --> 00:57.570
So 10 moves ago.

00:57.570 --> 01:00.345
I don't really know why I would do that, but you can.

01:00.345 --> 01:03.681
But we can also do things like

01:03.681 --> 01:08.681
let's say I wanna know what head looks like two moves ago.

01:09.450 --> 01:10.740
Well, I can do a checkout

01:10.740 --> 01:14.080
I can do git checkout head at

01:15.060 --> 01:17.010
two moves ago.

01:17.010 --> 01:19.983
Now this is different than this right here.

01:20.850 --> 01:24.283
We've seen this, this means the parent of head,

01:24.283 --> 01:28.770
and then its parent two generations of parents ago.

01:28.770 --> 01:31.410
So basically the grandparent commit.

01:31.410 --> 01:35.070
But if I do head at two,

01:35.070 --> 01:36.621
this may not even be commits

01:36.621 --> 01:39.990
that we are or parent commits that we're going back in time.

01:39.990 --> 01:42.169
We may be on the same exact commit

01:42.169 --> 01:44.220
and we just switch branches

01:44.220 --> 01:47.340
because head at two is just saying two moves ago

01:47.340 --> 01:49.350
in the ref log for head.

01:49.350 --> 01:50.910
So that might just be changing branches

01:50.910 --> 01:52.817
it might be detached head, who knows,

01:52.817 --> 01:54.540
and we could look it up.

01:54.540 --> 01:56.040
But this is what we're saying.

01:56.040 --> 01:59.520
Go back two entries ago for head. And,

01:59.520 --> 02:02.038
we go into detached head because we use git checkout

02:02.038 --> 02:06.420
and it says it took us to this commit D8363.

02:06.420 --> 02:07.620
So I'm gonna switch out.

02:09.000 --> 02:10.770
Nope, I can't have that zero.

02:10.770 --> 02:11.760
There we are.

02:11.760 --> 02:14.253
Let's do a git log one line.

02:15.180 --> 02:19.320
So if I did git checkout head tilde two,

02:19.320 --> 02:20.153
this is head,

02:20.153 --> 02:24.150
this is tilde one, that's tilde two, I go to F F E.

02:24.150 --> 02:27.150
Okay, that would be going back, right?

02:27.150 --> 02:30.240
Two commits a parent and a parent.

02:30.240 --> 02:33.873
So that is what this syntax would be tilde two.

02:35.490 --> 02:38.913
But again, if I do git ref log show head,

02:41.400 --> 02:43.680
if we look at what head two ago is,

02:43.680 --> 02:45.510
it's this commit,

02:45.510 --> 02:46.650
7 C 1.

02:46.650 --> 02:49.569
That is actually the same commit we're currently on

02:49.569 --> 02:52.560
because there's just an entry in the middle where we went

02:52.560 --> 02:55.470
to detached head, we came back, we switched branches.

02:55.470 --> 02:58.830
So it may not seem useful like this right?

02:58.830 --> 03:01.680
Why would I want to go back to the same place where I am?

03:01.680 --> 03:05.425
I'm on this commit now and I was on it two heads ago,

03:05.425 --> 03:07.620
why would I go back to that?

03:07.620 --> 03:09.840
But in the next video I will show you

03:09.840 --> 03:12.630
that we can actually do fancier things instead of

03:12.630 --> 03:14.580
just saying, you know, two moves ago

03:14.580 --> 03:18.140
I could say last week or yesterday or an hour ago.

03:18.140 --> 03:19.779
But also, this works

03:19.779 --> 03:23.012
with things other than head any of these references.

03:23.012 --> 03:26.760
We can specify an entry in a ref log using the same syntax.

03:26.760 --> 03:28.800
So master branch at five is

03:28.800 --> 03:32.490
five tips of the master branch ago.

03:32.490 --> 03:34.980
And we can pass these to other commands, too.

03:34.980 --> 03:36.293
For example, diff.

03:36.293 --> 03:38.850
If I wanted to know, you know, what's changed

03:38.850 --> 03:42.630
between head right now and head five moves ago.

03:42.630 --> 03:47.630
I could do git diff head right now would be zero.

03:47.910 --> 03:48.743
And then,

03:50.160 --> 03:52.030
head at, let's do five

03:53.310 --> 03:55.650
and I have to spell diff correctly with two F's.

03:55.650 --> 03:56.483
And there we are.

03:56.483 --> 03:57.540
We can see what's changed.

03:57.540 --> 03:58.373
Now if I did this

03:58.373 --> 04:02.070
with head zero and head two, nothing changed

04:02.070 --> 04:04.500
because they're referring to the same commit.

04:04.500 --> 04:07.350
Just two different things over that span.

04:07.350 --> 04:09.985
Again, I'll just recap one last time.

04:09.985 --> 04:12.072
git ref log show head.

04:12.072 --> 04:16.260
This is head zero, I'm on seven C one and head two.

04:16.260 --> 04:19.290
I'm on the same commit just in between.

04:19.290 --> 04:23.490
First I moved from donkey to detached head

04:23.490 --> 04:26.700
and then I moved from that detached head back to donkey.

04:26.700 --> 04:28.831
So the end result is the same.

04:28.831 --> 04:31.222
Okay, so this syntax will come up

04:31.222 --> 04:34.530
in the next video when I show you some fancier options.

04:34.530 --> 04:36.420
And it will definitely be important when we see how

04:36.420 --> 04:39.262
we can use ref logs to rescue ourselves

04:39.262 --> 04:41.523
from potentially bad situations.

04:41.523 --> 04:45.060
How we can access information that we might view as

04:45.060 --> 04:46.252
lost otherwise.

04:46.252 --> 04:50.133
Anyway, next up, we'll see these fancy timed references.
